-- title:
--   Unipotent equivariance and norm invariance of W_f on GL₃
-- statement:
--   Let $f\colon \mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}})\to\mathbb{C}$ be a function on the group of invertible $3\times 3$ matrices over the adele ring of $\mathbb{Q}$, assumed left invariant under the image of $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q})$ under the entrywise map induced by $\mathbb{Q}\to\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}$, i.e. $f(\gamma g)=f(g)$ for all $\gamma\in\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q})$ and all adelic $g$. Write $n(x,y,z)$ for the upper unitriangular matrix with entries $x$ in position $(1,2)$, $y$ in position $(2,3)$ and $z$ in position $(1,3)$, and let $\psi=$ `psiQ` be the global additive character of $\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ that is the product of the archimedean and finite standard characters. The Whittaker transform used is $W_f(g)=\int\!\int\!\int f(n(x,y,z)g)\,\psi(-(x+y))\,d\nu(z)\,d\nu(y)\,d\nu(x)$, where $\nu$ is the additive Haar measure of $\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ conditioned on the adelic box (the product of a fundamental domain for the Minkowski lattice at the archimedean place with the integral finite adeles); the remaining data of the carrier record — empty domain, trivial level subgroups, unit generators, full centre, and the Haar measure on $\mathrm{GL}_2$ of the adeles. The conclusion is the conjunction of two assertions: first, $W_f(n(x,y,z)g)=\psi(x+y)\,W_f(g)$ for all adeles $x,y,z$ and all $g$; second, $\lVert W_f(ng)\rVert=\lVert W_f(g)\rVert$ for every $g$ and every $n$ in the subgroup of $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}})$ generated by all the matrices $n(x,y,z)$.
--
--   This is the transformation law of a Whittaker function under left translation by the unipotent radical of the Borel subgroup of $\mathrm{GL}_3$: equivariance by the character $\psi(x+y)$ on the generating unitriangular matrices, and invariance of the absolute value on the whole unipotent subgroup they generate. It is the basic equivariance input for the later analysis of Whittaker expansions along the diagonal torus and for the vanishing and coefficient-equivariance statements that cite it.
